## UI input disposition

This is my first input on W-675; there are no earlier findings of mine to reassess. I judged round 5, brief sha256 `4d6b0ec914453e42a36b959d6bae79e9f53bf309fc5c77b4e3806df8d7d13505`.

I inspected all thirty-two captures and independently compared their decoded pixels: all sixteen pairs have zero differences. At build, re-check Board, Inbox, Officina and Seats at 1280×900 expanded and collapsed, 640×900 and 390×900, including text hierarchy, selection, evidence links, status labels and control boundaries. Establish the implementation’s identity with prototype tree `303b44f99deb5dcbf4d9fe1a96e8ae68dc2d4807` or regenerate the comparison against the implementation. Confirm advisories 1 and 2 are addressed, with browser evidence supporting the existing state checks beyond these initial captures.

## Recommendation

The extraction preserves legibility and all information visible in the captured states; I found no blocking UI defect.
The role names sensibly distinguish purple headings, cyan links and facts, pink categories and selection, yellow project names, neutral surfaces, and workflow versus severity indicators.
The compatibility aliases remain deliberately ambiguous: borders still follow `--rule` into the selected-surface role, and the Inbox’s cyan identifier still follows the link role; purpose-based consumer migration remains necessary before a whole-theme swap changes the right things independently.
Pixel preservation and deferred visual corrections are Patron rulings under D-217; the brief’s implementation proposals make sense for that bounded extraction, and I found no agent-written rule applied here that warrants a screen correction.
Verdict: passed